  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a squid swim so fast?",
      "a": "A squid fills its mantle with water, then squeezes it out through the siphon in a powerful jet. The water rushing one way pushes the squid the other way, so it can dart off in a flash.",
      "asks": [
        "How do squid jet?",
        "What is jet propulsion in animals?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a siphon on a squid?",
      "a": "The siphon, or funnel, is a muscular tube under the head. Water from the mantle is forced out through it, and the squid swivels it to aim the jet and steer.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a squid's funnel?",
        "How does a squid steer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do jellyfish move?",
      "a": "A jellyfish tightens the muscles around the rim of its bell, pushing water out behind it. The bell springs back like rubber and refills, and each pulse nudges it forward, though currents carry it too.",
      "asks": [
        "How do jellyfish swim?",
        "Can jellyfish swim or do they drift?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is jet propulsion like a rocket?",
      "a": "Both work by Newton's third law: push something out backwards and you are pushed forwards. A rocket throws out hot gas; a squid throws out water.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a squid like a rocket?",
